1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,200
Closed-loop LLM planners call the model at every corrective step.

2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,600
Oracle, executor, judge — five calls to take one step.

3
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:21,000
Over the benchmark that is fifty-four calls and two hundred and eleven seconds per task.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,600
FOLBP restructures the loop around a symbolic core.

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,600
One LLM call proposes the complete multi-robot plan.

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:31,400
The scene graph compiles to ground first-order atoms, with no model involved.

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:35,800
Certification forward-simulates the plan and checks every precondition.

8
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,600
A validator checks it against each platform's declared capabilities.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:41,000
Execution is a dispatch table. Only one block here is a language model.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,400
Certification returns the unsatisfied literals as a conflict core.

11
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,000
Bounded repair splices in the prerequisite — with no model call.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,200
Only conflicts no insertion can fix reach the proposer again.

13
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:54,800
Once certified and validated, the plan is dispatched — in simulation, or to the robots.

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,400
Reach the goal and the run is finished.

15
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,000
A runtime rejection is learned instead, and shapes the next proposal.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Both planners get the same scene graph and the same goal.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
FOLBP's plan is certified before the first step runs.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
PEFA decides one step at a time — five calls each.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
FOLBP has not called the model since the plan was certified.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Same robots, same eleven steps. Only the deliberation differs.

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
FOLBP: eleven of eleven, 326 seconds, one LLM call.

22
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
PEFA is still executing.

23
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
PEFA: the same eleven steps, 490 seconds, fifty-five calls.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,200
On real hardware the TurtleBot must put the ball into the drone's basket.

25
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:11,400
Move-towards brings the base partway; the approach finishes inside grab.

26
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:16,700
FOLBP already holds all seven steps. PEFA is still deciding.

27
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:21,000
FOLBP: ball in the basket, handing over to the drone.

28
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,100
FOLBP is done — seven of seven, basket down at setpoint A.

29
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:34,000
PEFA is still carrying the ball to the basket.

30
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,300
PEFA's drone lifts off.

31
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:45,034
Planning cost: one call against thirty-five, 8.2 seconds against 127.

32
00:02:45,034 --> 00:02:50,234
Across one hundred tasks and two models, success matches.

33
00:02:50,234 --> 00:02:55,234
The cost does not: twenty-five to forty-two times fewer calls.

34
00:02:55,234 --> 00:03:00,034
Twelve to twenty-one times fewer tokens, and nine to nineteen times lower latency.

